WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.830
Four clubs, three afternoons, and one

2
00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.730
student who’s in three of them.

3
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.480
Every term we argue about

4
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.080
which club meets when.

5
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.180
This term, let’s let a solver search.

6
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:18.800
Today: timetables with CP-SAT,

7
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.740
from Google’s OR-Tools.

8
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.310
Each club gets a variable: its

9
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:26.190
day, from one to three.

10
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.620
Each student’s clubs must all

11
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:29.840
be on different days.

12
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.220
And the football field is

13
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.340
busy on day three.

14
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:34.850
That’s the whole model.

15
00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.650
In Python, that’s a dictionary of day

16
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.370
variables, one all-different rule per

17
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.730
student, and one line for the field.

18
00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.850
The solver doesn’t try all

19
00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:47.810
eighty-one plans.

20
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.560
It crosses out days that can’t work,

21
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.140
guesses only when it’s stuck, and undoes

22
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.620
any guess that hits a dead end.

23
00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.090
That’s propagation and search.

24
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.300
In the lab’s school timetable,

25
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.260
propagation and search need eight guesses.

26
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.260
Plain backtracking needs three

27
00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:08.500
hundred and eighty-nine.

28
00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:10.830
Add one rule, and the

29
00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.170
search needs only two.

30
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.470
Sometimes the answer is INFEASIBLE, and

31
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.210
that’s a proof, not a failure.

32
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.860
Sara is in chess, drama and coding, so

33
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.640
she needs three different days.

34
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.630
Add a rule that no club meets on day

35
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.850
three, and only two days are left.

36
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.430
The solver proves no timetable exists.

37
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:35.250
Quick check!

38
00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.630
Our club model has no goal, and

39
00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.250
the solver says OPTIMAL.

40
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.700
What does OPTIMAL mean here?

41
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.120
With no goal, OPTIMAL just means a

42
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.160
timetable that keeps every rule.

43
00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.770
Not the fairest, and not the

44
00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:52.790
one students would pick.

45
00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:54.570
Only what you wrote down.

46
00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.520
So look at who carries the cost.

47
00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.550
If drama always gets the last

48
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.830
afternoon before the holiday, the

49
00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.490
same students always miss it.

50
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.940
Write fairness down, as a rule like

51
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.840
rotating days each term, and solve again.

52
00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.170
In the app, open “Build the timetable”.

53
00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.550
Press One step to watch

54
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.390
propagation, then Run.

55
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.080
Tick the extra rules one at a time, and

56
00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.540
watch the guesses fall, then the proof.

57
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.540
Your turn.

58
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.760
Your club model says INFEASIBLE.

59
00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:32.320
What should you do first?

60
00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.830
Find the rules that clash, and ask the

61
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.690
people involved which one can bend.

62
00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.190
Remember: a variable for each choice,

63
00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:46.070
a line for each rule, and the solver

64
00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:47.650
crosses out and searches.

65
00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.060
INFEASIBLE is a proof.

66
00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.420
And OPTIMAL only keeps

67
00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:53.560
the rules you wrote.

68
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.240
Try it yourself: model your study

69
00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.180
week, or your family’s chores,

70
00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:00.940
with the same few lines.

71
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:02.960
Who gets the worst slot?

72
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.710
Add a rule to share it, and solve again.
